course: dictionary
lesson: 11001
title: Meet the wasp
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can have clues all around it. «If a wasp comes to your drink, stay calm, cover the drink and move away slowly.» The words drink and sting tell you this wasp is a flying insect, not a person or a drink.
explain: A wasp is a flying insect with a thin waist and yellow and black stripes. When you read about one, look for these clues: stripes, a thin waist, buzzing, or a sting. Each one helps you picture the insect.
explain: Some words are about more than one thing. The word wasp can mean the insect, or the nest where many wasps live together in a colony. Read the words around it: «a wasps' nest» is about the home, while «a wasp sting» is about the sting.
fact: A wasp is a flying insect with a thin waist and yellow and black stripes.
fact: Wasps can sting, and unlike a honeybee, a wasp can sting more than once.
fact: Wasps live in nests, often made from chewed wood that looks like paper.
fact: «Bees and wasps sting mainly to defend themselves and their nest.»
fact: Many wasps live together in a colony, and some wasps help control pests by eating other insects.
exercise: «Wasps and bumblebees do not usually die when they sting and can sting more than once.» What does this tell us about wasps? | *They can sting more than once | They never sting | They sting only at night | They always die after one sting
exercise: Ben sees a yellow and black striped insect with a thin waist buzzing near his lemonade. What is it most likely to be? | A snail | A ladybird | *A wasp | A worm
exercise: «A hoverfly is a harmless fly with wasp-like yellow and black stripes.» What does wasp-like mean? | *Looking a bit like a wasp | Made of wasps | Stinging people all day long | Eaten by wasps
test: «If a wasp comes to your drink, stay calm, cover the drink and move away slowly.» Why should you not swat at a wasp? | It helps the wasp find its nest | Wasps like to be swatted | *Swatting and waving can make it sting | It makes wasps hungry for your drink
test: What are wasps' nests often made from? | Glass and metal | Sand and stones | Mud and leaves | *Chewed wood that looks like paper
exam: «Many wasps live together in a colony» and some eat other insects. Why are wasps useful to gardeners? | They make honey for the garden | They water the plants | They carry seeds across the sea | *They eat other insects that are pests
